from assessment_writer import write_review
def compact(sid,title,rows,appraisal):
    write_review(sid,title,[(a,b.split(' | '),c) for a,b,c in rows],appraisal)

compact('MF-PD-MECHANISM-2016','Parkinson-like toxin models: mechanistic plausibility, not human efficacy',[
('Objectives','Test neuronal protection and AMPK/autophagy mechanisms. | NR: No registered experimental protocol reported. | Toxin-induced neuronal injury and cell signaling.','Not idiopathic human Parkinson disease.'),
('Animals; cell culture; Figures 1–5','Male C57BL/6 mice and human-derived SH-SY5Y cells. | Ten-week-old mice; MPTP plus probenecid toxin model. | Behavioral/chemistry n=8 per group, histology n=4, MAO-B n=5; three independent cell experiments.','No human participants.'),
('Treatment','Metformin from Pacific Ocean, Tianjin. | Mice: 5 mg/mL drinking water from day three for five weeks; cells: 2 mM pretreatment one hour. | MPTP 20 mg/kg plus probenecid 250 mg/kg every 3.5 days; cells MPP+ 200 µM for 48 hours. | Mouse motor test day 37; cell assays up to 48 hours.','Water concentration is not a measured mg/kg intake or human-equivalent dose.'),
('Methods','Saline, toxin alone, toxin plus metformin and metformin alone. | Four mouse groups; cell inhibitor comparisons. | Mouse random allocation stated; sequence not described. | Stereological assessment blinded; other masking not clearly described. | Metformin during toxin exposure, not after longstanding disease.','Prevention/co-treatment differs from treating established degeneration.'),
('Assays','Motor, dopamine, neuron counts, inflammatory markers and signaling. | Rotarod, HPLC, histology, western blot, PCR and cell viability. | Five-week mouse course; short cell exposures. | Animal function and mechanistic surrogate outcomes. | NA: No validated human clinically meaningful threshold.','No human symptom or progression endpoint.'),
('Results; Figures 1,4,5','Toxin rotarod 24.7±8.0 versus toxin-plus-metformin 81.7±11.4, reported as minutes. | Eight mice per motor group; four per neuron-count group. | Reported 25% higher TH-positive neuron count with metformin versus toxin alone. | Dysfunctional mitochondria 19.4% toxin versus 8.5% with metformin, 3.4% untreated cells. | Means±SEM and ANOVA/Tukey P values; no clinical effect CI.','Do not convert laboratory proportions into patient risk reductions.'),
('Methods; figure legends','NR: Whole-experiment animal attrition not explicitly tabulated. | NR: Missing observations and exclusion criteria not fully documented. | NR: Water intake and achieved drug concentration not reported. | Different assays use different n. | Assay-specific animals or independent culture experiments. | Mouse or independent culture; sections/fields are technical observations.','Do not count 12 sections per brain as 12 animals.'),
('Methods; Results','No comprehensive toxicology program. | Small mouse groups and cell cultures only. | Metformin-only controls and viability assays do not measure human adverse effects. | NR: Toxicity-related animal withdrawals not reported. | Five weeks maximum in mice.','Cannot establish safety for older adults.'),
('Methods; Figures; Discussion','Small samples, limited masking detail and pharmacologic inhibitor specificity. | Randomized toxin model limits some confounding; no clinical disease heterogeneity. | NR: No accessible preregistered analysis plan. | Numerous correlated molecular endpoints; Tukey within analyses, not global correction. | Small n and no clinically transportable intervals. | Rotarod unit reported as minutes; implausibility warrants caution, no silent unit change.','Compound C/3MA reversal supports a hypothesis but does not prove a uniquely specific human mechanism.'),
('Discussion','Mouse toxin injury and a transformed cell line. | Millimolar cell exposure and unmeasured mouse intake do not establish human brain exposure. | Indirect rationale only. | Does not demonstrate disease modification or symptom benefit in patients.','The separate CP016 clinical trial is assessed on its own outcomes.'),
('Report identity','This mouse/cell experimental program. | Earlier animal work cited; CP016 is separate human evidence. | Figures within this paper are not independent clinical replications. | Scoped current guide inventory.','Multiple assays strengthen triangulation, not clinical certainty.'),
('Byline; acknowledgments','Ming Lu, Cunjin Su, Chen Qiao, Yaqi Bian, Jianhua Ding and Gang Hu. | Institutional animal and laboratory facilities in China. | National Natural Science Foundation of China, national science/technology and Jiangsu grants. | Commercial metformin/reagents identified. | Statement of Interest: none.','Authors’ proposed therapeutic promise remains interpretation.'),
('Full XML','Published 2016 experimental report. | Full text acquired and methods/results read. | No separate supplement relied upon. | NA: Not a registered human trial; animal ethics approval reported. | No correction/retraction identified in retrieved records. | November 3–4, 2026.','Targeted appraisal, not forensic image analysis.'),
('G-METFORMIN neurological mechanisms','Autophagy and mitochondrial hypotheses. | Indirect support for further research only. | Observed toxin-model protection; authors speculate about Parkinson therapy; guide makes no human-efficacy inference.','No disease-modifying claim.'),
('Synthesis','Interesting biological rationale with major model/exposure limits. | Human target engagement and durable clinical benefit unknown. | Adequate randomized human trials with functional outcomes and safety needed.','A mechanism is not a treatment result.')
], 'Small randomized toxin-model experiments and cell assays support a mechanistic hypothesis. They do not establish efficacy, a dose, safety or disease modification in humans.')

compact('MF-MYELOMA-MODELS-2018','Myeloma cell and xenograft experiments: preclinical evidence only',[
('Objectives','Test growth inhibition and AMPK/mTOR pathways. | NR: No preregistered experimental analysis plan reported. | Cell proliferation, cell cycle and xenograft growth.','No patient survival question was tested.'),
('Methods','Human myeloma cell lines RPMI8226/U266 and NOD/SCID mice. | Cell-bank lines; five-week-old immunodeficient mice with subcutaneous xenografts. | Twelve mice randomized, six per group; cell experiments in triplicate with repeats.','Human-derived cells are not human participants.'),
('Reagents; experimental methods','Sigma metformin dissolved in PBS. | Cells 5–80 mM, 24–72 hours; mice oral 250 mg/kg/day for 21 days. | AMPK siRNA, Compound C and 3-MA in mechanistic experiments. | Serial tumor volume/body weight every three days, terminal tissue at day 21.','No direct translation of these doses to a patient.'),
('Methods','Untreated/PBS controls; scrambled siRNA controls. | Two xenograft cohorts; assay-specific cell comparisons. | Mice randomly assigned; sequence unspecified. | NR: Blinded tumor measurement or analysis not reported. | Treatment after tumors reached approximately 0.5 by 0.5 cm.','Model is established xenograft growth, not naturally occurring myeloma.'),
('Assays','Viability, proliferation, cycle arrest, autophagy, pathway phosphorylation and tumor size. | CCK8, EdU, flow cytometry, electron microscopy, western blot and calipers. | Hours in cells, three weeks in mice. | Laboratory surrogates, no clinical response or survival. | NA: No patient-important change threshold.','Subcutaneous tumor volume is not a validated surrogate for patient survival here.'),
('Results; Figures 1–6','48-hour IC50 20.2±1.2 mM RPMI8226 and 17.9±1.1 mM U266. | Cell triplicates; xenograft six mice per group. | Mean±SD; lower tumor growth reported in Figure 6. | G0/G1 arrest and reduced proliferation, not increased apoptosis. | NR: Exact tumor-volume contrast/CI not tabulated; no digitized estimate claimed.','Qualitative tumor direction is used, not invented bar-chart precision.'),
('Methods; figures','NR: Animal flow/attrition not explicitly tabulated. | NR: Missing values and exclusion rules not fully described. | Daily gavage stated; no measured plasma concentration. | Cell-line and assay selection limits scope. | Assay-specific cultures and twelve mice. | Independent culture or animal, not image field.','Triplicates and repeated fields do not create patient counts.'),
('Xenograft results','Body weight monitored as a limited toxicity indicator. | Six metformin-treated mice. | No significant body-weight difference reported. | NR: Toxicity-related withdrawals not specified. | Twenty-one days.','Stable weight is not comprehensive toxicology or proof of human safety.'),
('Methods; Discussion','Limited animal n, unclear masking, cell-line dependence and high concentrations. | Knockdown plus drug inhibitors provide triangulation but have specificity limitations. | NR: No experimental preregistration identified. | Many t-tests across doses/timepoints/pathways without a stated global adjustment. | No clinical effect estimate or uncertainty. | Figure 5 legend duplicates P<.05 for one/two stars; model Figure 6 says triplicate despite six mice per group.','Do not overinterpret significance symbols or replicate descriptions.'),
('Discussion','Preclinical myeloma models. | Millimolar culture exposure and mouse gavage do not establish achievable human tumor exposure. | Rationale for research, not treatment benefit. | No evidence here of reduced myeloma mortality or safe combination therapy.','Authors’ “approved and safe” language refers to another indication, not this experimental use.'),
('Paper identity','2018 myeloma laboratory study. | External observational reports are background citations, not its own patient data. | Multiple assay panels belong to one experimental program. | Current guide inventory only.','Do not count background reports without assessment.'),
('Byline; acknowledgments','Yan Wang, Wenbin Xu, Zixun Yan, Weili Zhao, Jianqing Mi, Junmin Li and Hua Yan. | Shanghai Jiao Tong University-related animal/laboratory setting. | National Natural Science Foundation of China grants 81302038 and 81600155. | Commercial Sigma drug and named assay suppliers. | Authors declared no competing interests.','No inference about validity from funding alone.'),
('Full article','Published 2018, Journal of Experimental & Clinical Cancer Research. | Full XML acquired and inspected. | No separate supplementary result used. | NA: Animal ethics reported; no human trial registration. | No correction/retraction identified in retrieved records. | November 3–4, 2026.','Not a forensic assessment of blot images.'),
('G-METFORMIN myeloma section','Antimyeloma mechanisms. | Indirect laboratory support only. | Growth inhibition observed; clinical promise is authors’ extrapolation.','No clinical efficacy claim.'),
('Synthesis','Laboratory activity is insufficient to recommend metformin for myeloma. | Human exposure, efficacy and combination safety unknown. | Direct controlled human trials with patient outcomes required.','Keep the preclinical label prominent.')
], 'Cell and xenograft activity supplies a hypothesis, not clinical efficacy. High cell concentrations, small unblinded animal comparisons and limited toxicology prevent therapeutic extrapolation.')

compact('INZUCCHI-2014','Kidney safety review: historical context, superseded prescribing rules',[
('Objective; search','Review lactic acidosis risk in metformin users with impaired kidney function. | Published search criteria; no review registration reported. | Safety and possible prescribing-policy change.','This is a 2014 review, not current labeling.'),
('Literature search','Human type 2 diabetes/CKD studies. | MEDLINE and Cochrane, English-language January 1950–June 2014. | 818 records to 65 reports: ten pharmacokinetic, twenty case series, thirty-one observational/surveillance, three meta-analyses and one trial.','Report counts are not unique participant counts.'),
('Included evidence','Metformin; formulations/doses varied across studies. | Oral clinical use and pharmacokinetic exposure. | Other glucose-lowering treatment and concurrent illnesses varied. | Heterogeneous study periods and follow-up.','No single regimen or exposure is tested by this review.'),
('Methods; evidence summaries','Other therapies, nonuse or kidney-function strata depending on study. | Review-level study selection, not patient allocation. | Predominantly nonrandomized evidence. | NA: No common masking method across included designs. | Search completed July 2014.','Observational associations cannot establish causal safety equivalence.'),
('Results','Lactic acidosis, lactate, metformin levels and clinical outcomes. | Definitions varied; some code-based, some laboratory-confirmed. | Person-year incidence in cohorts; short pharmacokinetic sampling. | Serious clinical harm versus intermediate lactate elevations. | Lactic acidosis definition: lactate above 5 mmol/L and pH below 7.35.','Elevated lactate alone is not the same event.'),
('Results, trials/retrospective studies','Typical reported rates around 3–10 per 100,000 person-years; some higher. | Cited meta-analysis: zero events over 70,490 metformin and 55,451 comparator person-years. | Upper 95% incidence limits 4.3 and 5.4 per 100,000 person-years. | Observational estimates conflict; one CKD analysis HR 6.37, CI 1.48–27.5. | Sparse events and broad CIs limit conclusions for impaired kidneys.','Zero observed events is not zero risk.'),
('Methods; limitations','Study-level attrition varied and was not pooled. | Kidney function missing in more than 25% in one large cited cohort. | Prescribing records do not ensure ingestion. | Excluded small case series, nonhuman studies and non-English evidence. | Narrative synthesis of heterogeneous reports. | Primary study or cohort; overlapping databases possible.','No de novo unique-person denominator.'),
('Safety synthesis','Clinical codes, spontaneous reports and laboratory diagnoses varied. | No unified exposed denominator across 65 reports. | Rare but potentially fatal acidosis; competing acute illness often present. | No pooled withdrawal result. | Study-specific durations.','Detection and attribution are substantial limitations.'),
('Discussion','Confounding by indication, healthier-user selection and surveillance bias. | Acute kidney injury, sepsis and hypoxia can cause both metformin accumulation and acidosis. | GAP: Supplementary PRISMA details not separately acquired; no historical protocol audit. | Narrative review, not a new pooled causal estimate. | Rare-event estimates unstable in severe CKD. | 2014 creatinine-based FDA restrictions and proposed dosing table are historical, not current policy.','Do not copy old prescribing rules into the guide.'),
('Conclusions; current labels','Diabetes with CKD, not healthy longevity use. | Clinical metformin exposure; no evidence for experimental high-dose use. | Supports kidney-function monitoring and contextualizes rare harm. | Cannot establish safety during acute illness or eGFR below current contraindication threshold.','Current product labeling supplies current precautions.'),
('Included studies','Systematic review of mixed designs. | Some shared national databases and nested reviews. | Overlap must not be counted as independent trials. | Search scope fixed at June 2014.','Not a comprehensive 2026 safety update.'),
('Byline; acknowledgments','Silvio Inzucchi, Kasia Lipska, Helen Mayo, Clifford Bailey and Darren McGuire. | Academic diabetes/clinical research authors. | Investigator NIH/Yale support disclosed; no new trial funded. | NA: No product administration by reviewers. | Multiple consulting, honoraria, travel and industry relationships disclosed.','Disclosures do not determine the scientific conclusion.'),
('Full report','JAMA 2014 systematic review. | Full text acquired. | GAP: Separate supplemental flow diagram not acquired. | NR: Review protocol registration not reported. | No correction identified in retrieved records; regulatory statements superseded by current labels. | November 3–4, 2026.','Use only as dated historical safety context.'),
('G-METFORMIN safety','Rare-harm interpretation and renal clearance. | Contextual support, not authority to override current contraindications. | Authors proposed expanded access; guide uses current FDA-label thresholds.','No unsupported reassurance of zero acidosis risk.'),
('Synthesis','Kidney function and acute illness are central to safe use. | Rare-event risk in severe CKD remained uncertain in this review. | Current high-quality safety data and updated labeling govern present clinical decisions.','Do not individualize dose from this historical article.')
], 'A useful historical synthesis with explicit limitations from rare events, heterogeneous definitions and confounding. It is unsuitable as current prescribing authority; current labeling supersedes its 2014 regulatory description.')
